    "content": "# Surah An-Naba (النبأ) — The Great News\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah An-Naba**. This Surah forms an essential part of **Juz 'Amma (The 30th Part of the Holy Qur'an)**, revealed to awaken human conscience, establish the reality of eternal accountability, and ground the believer's daily life in sincere devotion to Allah.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 78 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة النبأ |\n| **English Meaning** | The Great News |\n| **Classification** | Makki Revelation |\n| **Total Verses** | 40 Verses |\n| **Core Theme** | The Day of Sorting (Yawm al-Fasl), cosmic proofs of resurrection in nature, the torment of Hellfire, and the ultimate triumph of the righteous. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Revelation & Circumstances (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed in Makkah when the pagan Quraysh began fiercely disputing among themselves concerning the resurrection and the Prophet's ﷺ warning of eternal judgment.\n\nScholars of Tafsir highlight that understanding the historical moment of revelation provides indispensable context for understanding why specific metaphors, warnings, and promises were selected by divine wisdom.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 1–5: The Great News and the Skeptics' Disputation\n\nThe polytheists of Makkah disputed vehemently concerning the truth of the resurrection and the final accounting. Allah asserts that their denial will soon be shattered by undeniable reality.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 6–16: Cosmic Testimonies of Divine Power in Creation\n\nAllah directs humanity to observe the cradle of the earth, the mountains as stabilizing pegs (Awtada), the blessing of sleep, the alternating night and day, the heavens, and torrential rainfall (Thajjaja) producing abundant vegetation.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 17–30: The Day of Sorting (Yawm al-Fasl) and the Reality of Hellfire\n\nThe appointed time when the Trumpet is blown, the skies are opened like gates, and the mountains are moved like a mirage. Transgressors enter Jahannam to taste boiling fluid (Hamim) and pus (Ghassaq) for endless ages (Ahqaba).\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 31–36: The Eternal Triumph of the Righteous (Al-Muttaqun)\n\nThe triumphant reward for those conscious of Allah: lush enclosed gardens, vineyards, youthful peers of equal age, and brimming cups of pure delight, bestowed as a generous divine compensation.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 37–40: Standing in Awe Before the Most Merciful\n\nThe Day when the Spirit (Jibreel) and the angels stand ranked in majestic silence, speaking only with permission of Ar-Rahman. The disbeliever will witness the deeds of his hands and cry: 'Oh, I wish I were mere dust!'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances\n\n- **An-Naba al-'Azim (النَّبَإِ العَظِيم)**: The Monumental News—the resurrection, final accountability, and truth of divine revelation.\n- **Mihada (مِهَٰدًا)**: A comfortable, smooth expanse spread out for living (referring to the earth).\n- **Awtada (أَوْتَادًا)**: Pegs or stabilizers anchored deep into the crust (describing the mountains).\n- **Thajjaja (ثَجَّاجًا)**: Abundantly pouring down in torrents (describing torrential rain).\n- **Ahqaba (أَحْقَابًا)**: Countless epochs and endless successive ages.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah An-Naba transform our real-world character and daily relationships, classical scholars encourage tailoring its lessons across developmental stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Allah created the beautiful earth, sleep for rest, and clouds for rain; always thank Allah for His gifts.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Do not follow the crowd in doubting truth; understand that every action is recorded and justice will be served.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Live with acute awareness of the Day of Sorting (Yawm al-Fasl); align worldly investments with eternal accountability.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Reflect on nature's miracles (mountains, rain, night and day) during daily routines.**: Reflect on nature's miracles (mountains, rain, night and day) during daily routines.\n2. **Review daily habits and eliminate actions you would dread seeing on the Day of Judgment.**: Review daily habits and eliminate actions you would dread seeing on the Day of Judgment.\n3. **Recite Surah An-Naba in voluntary night prayer to instill awe and mindfulness of the Hereafter.**: Recite Surah An-Naba in voluntary night prayer to instill awe and mindfulness of the Hereafter.\n",
