    "content": "# Surah Al-Qadr (القدر) — The Decree\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Qadr**. This Surah forms an essential part of **Juz 'Amma (The 30th Part of the Holy Qur'an)**, revealed to awaken human conscience, establish the reality of eternal accountability, and ground the believer's daily life in sincere devotion to Allah.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 97 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة القدر |\n| **English Meaning** | The Decree |\n| **Classification** | Makki Revelation |\n| **Total Verses** | 5 Verses |\n| **Core Theme** | The descent of the Qur'an on Laylat al-Qadr (The Night of Decree), its value superior to a thousand months (83+ years of continuous worship), and the descent of angels bearing divine peace until dawn. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Revelation & Circumstances (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed when the Prophet ﷺ mentioned past righteous men from previous nations who worshipped Allah for hundreds of years; Allah blessed his Ummah with a single annual night surpassing an entire lifetime of devotion.\n\nScholars of Tafsir highlight that understanding the historical moment of revelation provides indispensable context for understanding why specific metaphors, warnings, and promises were selected by divine wisdom.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 1–3: The Revelation on the Night of Destiny (Laylat al-Qadr)\n\nIndeed, We sent the Qur'an down during the Night of Decree. And what can make you know what is the Night of Decree? The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months of worship.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 4–5: The Descent of Angels and the Spirit: Peace Until Dawn\n\nThe angels and the Spirit (Jibreel) descend therein by permission of their Lord for every matter. Peace it is until the emergence of dawn—a night of immense mercy and spiritual safety.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances\n\n- **Laylat al-Qadr (لَيْلَةِ ٱلْقَدْرِ)**: The Night of Decree, Honor, Majesty, and Destiny.\n- **Khayrun min Alfi Shahr (خَيْرٌ مِّنْ أَلْفِ شَهْرٍ)**: Superior in blessing and reward to 1,000 months (over 83 years and 4 months).\n- **Tanazzalu al-Mala'ikah (تَنَزَّلُ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ)**: The angels descend in successive multitudes, filling every space on earth.\n- **Ar-Ruh (وَٱلرُّوحُ)**: The Archangel Jibreel (Gabriel), honored with individual mention due to his exalted rank.\n- **Salamun Hiya (سَلَٰمٌ هِىَ)**: It is absolute peace, security, and salvation from all evil until the break of dawn.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Qadr transform our real-world character and daily relationships, classical scholars encourage tailoring its lessons across developmental stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** During the last ten nights of Ramadan, pray and read Qur'an; one night's prayer is worth more than 83 years of rewards!\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Seize opportunities for massive spiritual reward; don't waste Ramadan nights scrolling on social media.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Dedicate the odd nights of Ramadan to intense repentance and Dua: 'Allahumma innaka 'afuwwun tuhibbu al-'afwa fa'fu 'anni'.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Seek Laylat al-Qadr during the odd nights of the last ten nights of Ramadan (21st, 23rd, 25th, 27th, 29th).**: Seek Laylat al-Qadr during the odd nights of the last ten nights of Ramadan (21st, 23rd, 25th, 27th, 29th).\n2. **Recite the Sunnah supplication for forgiveness taught by the Prophet ﷺ to Aisha (RA).**: Recite the Sunnah supplication for forgiveness taught by the Prophet ﷺ to Aisha (RA).\n3. **Reflect on how the descent of the Qur'an transformed human destiny from darkness into light.**: Reflect on how the descent of the Qur'an transformed human destiny from darkness into light.\n",
