    "content": "# Surah Al-Falaq (الفلق) — The Daybreak\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Falaq**. This Surah forms an essential part of **Juz 'Amma (The 30th Part of the Holy Qur'an)**, revealed to awaken human conscience, establish the reality of eternal accountability, and ground the believer's daily life in sincere devotion to Allah.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 113 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الفلق |\n| **English Meaning** | The Daybreak |\n| **Classification** | Makki Revelation |\n| **Total Verses** | 5 Verses |\n| **Core Theme** | Seeking comprehensive refuge in the Lord of Daybreak from all created evil, nighttime perils, the dark craft of sorcery, and the toxic harm of malicious envy (Hasad). |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Revelation & Circumstances (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed alongside Surah An-Nas when a Jewish sorcerer named Labid ibn al-A'sam attempted to cast a binding black magic knot against the Prophet ﷺ, and Angel Jibreel brought these two Surahs (Al-Mu'awwidhatayn) as divine spiritual cures.\n\nScholars of Tafsir highlight that understanding the historical moment of revelation provides indispensable context for understanding why specific metaphors, warnings, and promises were selected by divine wisdom.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 1–2: Seeking Refuge in the Lord of Daybreak from Created Evil\n\nSay, 'I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak from the evil of that which He created.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 3–5: Refuge from Settling Darkness, Sorcery, and the Envier\n\nAnd from the evil of darkness when it settles, from the evil of the blowers in knots, and from the evil of an envier when he envies.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Exegesis:** Classical commentaries (At-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di) emphasize that these verses establish the unyielding moral responsibility of the human soul before its Creator, stripping away worldly distractions.\n\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances\n\n- **Al-Falaq (ٱلْفَلَقِ)**: The Daybreak—the splitting of dawn out of the darkness of night.\n- **Min Sharri Ma Khalaq (مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ)**: From the evil of whatever He has created that possesses the capacity for harm.\n- **Ghasiqin Idha Waqab (غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ)**: Darkness as it settles, gathers, and fully engulfs.\n- **An-Naffathati fi al-'Uqad (ٱلنَّفَّٰثَٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ)**: Those who blow upon knots—practitioners of witchcraft, spells, and occult binding.\n- **Hasidin Idha Hasad (حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ)**: An envier when he acts upon and unleashes his destructive envy (Hasad).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Falaq transform our real-world character and daily relationships, classical scholars encourage tailoring its lessons across developmental stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** When you are scared of the dark or bad dreams, recite Surah Al-Falaq; Allah protects us from everything scary.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Protect yourself from envy (Hasad) and the evil eye ('Ayn); do not post your every blessing to provoke jealousy.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Recognize that envy is a corrosive poison that burns good deeds; seek refuge in Allah from both outer harms and internal bitterness.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Recite the Mu'awwidhatayn into your cupped hands every night before sleep, blow into them, and wipe over your body as the Prophet ﷺ did.**: Recite the Mu'awwidhatayn into your cupped hands every night before sleep, blow into them, and wipe over your body as the Prophet ﷺ did.\n2. **When you observe a blessing in someone else's life, immediately say 'BarakAllahu lak' (May Allah bless it for you) to safeguard against envy.**: When you observe a blessing in someone else's life, immediately say 'BarakAllahu lak' (May Allah bless it for you) to safeguard against envy.\n3. **Eliminate reliance on un-Islamic amulets, blue eyes, or superstitions; rely exclusively upon Qur'anic Adhkar.**: Eliminate reliance on un-Islamic amulets, blue eyes, or superstitions; rely exclusively upon Qur'anic Adhkar.\n",
